D = "ivx_dynamic"
ITEMS = [
dict(d=D, c="ivx malan triangle of conflict", m="eye", l=2,
q="A man describes feeling a flash of irritation at his boss, then suddenly gets a headache and says, “Anyway, he’s probably right.” Using Malan’s triangle of conflict, which mapping is correct?",
a="Irritation is the feeling or impulse, the headache is anxiety, and “he’s probably right” is the defense",
ae="Malan’s triangle of conflict links hidden feeling or impulse (F/I), the anxiety it evokes (A), and the defense (D) that keeps the feeling out of awareness. Somatic anxiety and capitulation are classic defensive sequences. Interview attention shifts back toward the feeling at the base of the triangle.",
w=[("The boss is the transference figure, the headache is the past, and the agreement is the present",
"This confuses the triangle of conflict with the triangle of person, which links Other (current), Transference and Past (parents)."),
("Irritation is a defense against sadness, the headache is the impulse, and the agreement is insight",
"Irritation may sometimes defend against sadness, but in this sequence it is the emerging feeling, and the agreement is a defensive retreat, not insight."),
("All three elements are anxiety equivalents and should be treated with relaxation techniques",
"This collapses the structure that gives the triangle its clinical power.")],
x="Malan’s two triangles (Individual Psychotherapy and the Science of Psychodynamics, 1979) are the backbone of short-term dynamic psychotherapy. The triangle of person adds where the pattern occurs: with current others (O), the therapist (T) and past figures (P). A T-C-P (or T-O-P) link, connecting the pattern across all three corners, is the most powerful interpretation.",
s="Malan, Individual Psychotherapy and the Science of Psychodynamics (1979)",
h="Conflict triangle: “Feel it → Fear it → Fend it off.” Person triangle: “Them now, me here, them then.”"),

dict(d=D, c="ivx davanloo central dynamic sequence", m="say", l=3, hard=True,
q="In Davanloo’s Intensive Short-Term Dynamic Psychotherapy (ISTDP), the trial therapy follows a central dynamic sequence. After the patient keeps giving vague answers about feelings, which intervention marks the phase of pressure?",
a="“What is the feeling you have toward him, right now, as you describe it?”",
ae="Pressure in ISTDP is the persistent focus on specific feelings, in the here and now and in specific episodes. It mobilizes complex transference feelings and defenses, which can then be challenged. Davanloo’s sequence is roughly: inquiry, pressure, challenge, transference resistance, direct access to the unconscious (breakthrough), systematic analysis, and dynamic exploration.",
w=[("“It seems this is hard for you; shall we come back to it later?”",
"This is supportive and may be appropriate in some contexts, but it releases the pressure rather than applying it."),
("“You keep being vague. Is that how you deal with everyone who gets close to you?”",
"This is closer to challenge or head-on collision with the resistance, which follows pressure. It also risks being heard as criticism if offered before pressure has mobilized feeling."),
("“Tell me about your childhood relationship with your father.”",
"This is inquiry into the past. In ISTDP the link to the past is made later, after feelings have been experienced in the present.")],
x="ISTDP emphasizes careful monitoring of unconscious anxiety pathways: striated muscle tension (sighing, hand clenching) suggests the patient can tolerate more pressure; smooth muscle discharge (gastrointestinal symptoms, headaches) and cognitive-perceptual disruption (blurred vision, confusion) suggest the need to restructure defenses and lower anxiety first. Allan Abbass has expanded this approach for fragile patients.",
s="Davanloo, Unlocking the Unconscious (1990); Abbass, Reaching Through Resistance (2015)",
h="Davanloo’s ladder: Inquire → Press → Challenge → Break through. “Ask, then lean, then name the wall.”"),

dict(d=D, c="ivx istdp anxiety pathways", m="eye", l=3, hard=True,
q="In an ISTDP trial session, as you press for feelings toward her ex-husband, the patient becomes foggy, says her vision is blurry and loses track of what you asked. What does this indicate and what follows?",
a="Cognitive-perceptual disruption: anxiety is above the threshold of tolerance, so reduce pressure and help her regulate before going further",
ae="Davanloo and Abbass describe pathways of unconscious anxiety discharge. Striated muscle tension (hand clenching, sighing) indicates anxiety within tolerance. Smooth muscle (bladder, gut, migraine) and cognitive-perceptual disruption (confusion, blurry vision, dissociation) signal anxiety above tolerance. The therapist then lowers anxiety through intellectualized recapping and building capacity before resuming pressure.",
w=[("Striated muscle anxiety: a good sign that you should increase pressure",
"Striated muscle tension looks different: clenched hands, sighing, muscle tension in limbs. Fog and blurred vision are not this pathway."),
("A breakthrough of unconscious feeling that should be encouraged",
"A breakthrough involves vivid experiencing of previously avoided feelings, not confusion and perceptual disruption."),
("Resistance in the form of deliberate evasion, requiring a head-on challenge",
"These symptoms are signs of anxiety beyond capacity, not willful resistance. Challenging would worsen the dysregulation.")],
x="This monitoring scheme is one of the most useful clinical tools from ISTDP, even for non-ISTDP interviewers. It turns the body into a gauge of how much exploration a patient can tolerate at each moment. Fragile patients need graded format, building capacity before pressure.",
s="Abbass, Reaching Through Resistance (2015); Davanloo (1990)",
h="Anxiety pathways: “Muscle = go, Gut = slow, Fog = stop.”"),

dict(d=D, c="ivx sifneos stapp criteria", m="ask", l=3, hard=True,
q="Peter Sifneos developed Short-Term Anxiety-Provoking Psychotherapy (STAPP). Which feature belonged to his selection criteria?",
a="Motivation for change in oneself, not merely for symptom relief",
ae="Sifneos’ criteria included a circumscribed chief complaint, at least one meaningful relationship in early life, the ability to interact flexibly with the evaluator and express feelings, above-average psychological sophistication, and motivation for change, not just symptom relief. He gave detailed criteria for assessing motivation, including honesty and willingness to make reasonable sacrifices.",
w=[("Severe, chronic symptoms that have not responded to medication",
"Sifneos selected patients with circumscribed problems; severe chronic disorders were usually excluded."),
("Absence of any meaningful relationships, which makes the therapist’s relationship central",
"Sifneos required at least one meaningful early relationship, as evidence of the capacity to relate."),
("High dependency on others, to ensure strong attachment to the therapist",
"Strong dependency was usually viewed as a contraindication to brief, anxiety-provoking work.")],
x="Sifneos also coined the term alexithymia (1973), from observing psychosomatic patients who could not find words for feelings. Brief dynamic therapy selection criteria (Sifneos, Malan, Mann, Davanloo) all assess, in the first interview, the patient’s capacity to tolerate anxiety and use interpretation. Malan’s practice of offering a trial interpretation and watching the response is the clearest test.",
s="Sifneos, Short-Term Dynamic Psychotherapy: Evaluation and Technique (1979)",
h="Sifneos: “Change ME, not just my SYMPTOM.” He also named alexithymia: “no words for feelings.”"),

dict(d=D, c="ivx malan trial interpretation", m="eye", l=3, hard=True,
q="In Malan’s brief dynamic assessment, what is the purpose of a trial interpretation in the first interview?",
a="To see whether the patient can use an interpretation to deepen rapport and produce new material",
ae="Malan observed that the response to a trial interpretation predicts suitability for brief dynamic therapy: patients who respond with new associations, feeling and increased rapport do well, while those who respond with intellectual agreement, withdrawal or worsening may need other approaches.",
w=[("To test whether the patient is psychotic by seeing if they become disorganized",
"Kernberg uses interpretation partly as a structural probe; Malan’s focus was on suitability for brief therapy."),
("To produce immediate insight and cure in a single session",
"Malan did not expect cure from one interpretation. It is a test of responsiveness."),
("To demonstrate the therapist’s expertise so the patient trusts the treatment",
"Showing expertise may be a side effect, but the trial interpretation is a diagnostic probe of the patient’s capacity.")],
x="Malan’s Tavistock studies of brief psychotherapy (with Balint) identified that therapist ability to focus, patient motivation and a T-P (transference-parent) link predicted success. The trial interpretation operationalizes Freud’s old question: “Can this person use this method?”",
s="Malan, A Study of Brief Psychotherapy (1963); Individual Psychotherapy and the Science of Psychodynamics",
h="Trial interpretation = “a sample bite.” If they ask for more, they can digest the meal."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx ccrt components", m="ask", l=2,
q="Luborsky’s Core Conflictual Relationship Theme (CCRT) is derived from relationship episodes told in sessions. What are its three components?",
a="The wish, the response of the other, and the response of the self",
ae="CCRT method extracts from narratives the person’s main wish (W), the expected or actual response from others (RO) and the resulting response of the self (RS). For example: W = to be close; RO = rejects me; RS = I withdraw and feel worthless. Repeated patterns across episodes define the core theme.",
w=[("The impulse, the anxiety, and the defense",
"This is Malan’s triangle of conflict, which focuses on intrapsychic sequence, not narrative relationship episodes."),
("The past, the present, and the transference",
"These are corners of Malan’s triangle of person."),
("Thought, feeling, and behavior",
"This is the CBT triad, not the CCRT.")],
x="Interview implication: ask for relationship episodes, specific stories of interactions (“Tell me about a time you and your partner had a disagreement”). The CCRT can be identified from about ten such episodes. It also tends to appear in the transference. Luborsky’s supportive-expressive therapy used the CCRT as the focus.",
s="Luborsky & Crits-Christoph, Understanding Transference: The CCRT Method (1990)",
h="CCRT: “I Want, They Do, I Do.” W-RO-RS."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx control mastery passive into active", m="eye", l=3, hard=True,
q="According to Weiss and Sampson’s control-mastery theory, a patient who was constantly criticized by her father begins to criticize you harshly in the second session. She is testing you. Which test is this?",
a="A passive-into-active test: she does to you what was done to her, to see if you can bear it without being crushed",
ae="Control-mastery theory describes two main types of tests. In transference tests, the patient behaves as they did with a parent to see if you respond differently from the parent. In passive-into-active tests, the patient does to the therapist what was done to them, hoping the therapist will show a healthier way of coping than they managed as a child.",
w=[("A transference test: she behaves as she did as a child to see if you criticize her as her father did",
"In a transference test she would act as she did as a child (submissive, inviting criticism). Here she takes the father’s critical role."),
("A resistance to treatment that should be interpreted as hostility",
"Control-mastery theory views testing as purposeful and unconscious work toward health. Interpreting it as hostility may fail the test."),
("Projective identification requiring you to set firm limits immediately",
"Projective identification is related conceptually, but control-mastery frames it as a test of whether you can model healthy coping.")],
x="Passing the test means showing in practice what the patient could not do as a child: staying calm, not collapsing, not retaliating, perhaps setting a gentle limit. Patients typically become more productive after a test is passed. The Plan Formulation Method (Curtis & Silberschatz) assesses goals, obstructions (pathogenic beliefs), tests and insights from the first interviews.",
s="Weiss, How Psychotherapy Works (1993); Weiss & Sampson, The Psychoanalytic Process (1986)",
h="Two tests: Transference = “I play child, will you play father?” Passive-into-active = “I play father, can you survive being me?”"),

dict(d=D, c="ivx pathogenic beliefs plan", m="ask", l=3, hard=True,
q="In control-mastery theory, what does the interviewer try to identify in the first sessions?",
a="The patient’s unconscious plan: goals, pathogenic beliefs obstructing them, the tests they will pose, and the insights they need",
ae="The Plan Formulation Method (Curtis, Silberschatz, Sampson, Weiss) identifies goals, obstructions (pathogenic beliefs such as “if I succeed, I will hurt my mother”), anticipated tests, and the insights that would help. Treatment is guided by whether interventions are pro-plan or anti-plan.",
w=[("The patient’s strongest resistances so they can be confronted early",
"Control-mastery theory does not frame early work as confronting resistances. It focuses on helping the patient disconfirm pathogenic beliefs."),
("The patient’s attachment classification from the AAI",
"Attachment classification is a different construct and method."),
("The diagnostic criteria the patient meets so that a manual can be selected",
"This is a categorical approach. Control-mastery formulation is individualized.")],
x="Pathogenic beliefs often concern guilt: survivor guilt (“I don’t deserve more than my siblings”) or separation guilt (“If I become independent, my mother will collapse”). Interview questions that touch these: “What would it mean to your family if you succeeded?” and “Who would be hurt if you were happy?”",
s="Curtis & Silberschatz, Plan Formulation Method; Silberschatz (ed.), Transformative Relationships (2005)",
h="Plan Formulation: “Goal, Ghost belief, Test, Truth needed.”"),

dict(d=D, c="ivx evenly suspended attention", m="eye", l=2,
q="Freud recommended a listening stance for analysts in his 1912 technical papers. Which description matches it?",
a="Evenly suspended (or hovering) attention: giving equal notice to everything without directing attention to particular material",
ae="Freud advised the analyst to listen with gleichschwebende Aufmerksamkeit, not selecting or concentrating, because focusing on what seems important leads to finding only what one already expects. It complements the patient’s free association.",
w=[("Focused attention on symptoms that meet diagnostic criteria",
"This is the descriptive diagnostic stance, which Freud saw as limiting in analytic listening."),
("Active attention to resistances, interrupting whenever they appear",
"Attention to resistance became central later (Reich, ego psychology), but the 1912 recommendation is about even hovering attention."),
("Attention to the analyst’s own countertransference only",
"Freud at that time saw countertransference mainly as an obstacle; its use as an instrument came later with Heimann and Racker.")],
x="Theodor Reik extended this in Listening with the Third Ear (1948), emphasizing the analyst’s unconscious receptivity. Bion’s later advice to approach each session without memory or desire radicalizes the same stance. In first interviews, a balance is needed: diagnostic focus is necessary, but some time with evenly suspended attention catches what the checklist misses.",
s="Freud, Recommendations to Physicians Practising Psycho-Analysis (1912)",
h="Evenly suspended = “a hovering hawk, not a diving one.”"),

dict(d=D, c="ivx lacan rectification subjective", m="say", l=3, hard=True,
q="In Lacan’s account of preliminary interviews, “subjective rectification” refers to which move?",
a="Questioning the patient’s position in the disorder they complain of, as Freud did with Dora: what is your own part in this?",
ae="Lacan, in The Direction of the Treatment (1958), described Freud asking Dora about her own part in the disorder she complained of. Subjective rectification shifts the patient from complaining about others to questioning their own involvement and desire, which establishes the conditions for analysis.",
w=[("Correcting the patient’s factual errors about their history",
"Rectification here is not factual correction. It concerns the subject’s position."),
("Adjusting the diagnosis after the first interview",
"Diagnosis matters in preliminary interviews, but rectification is about the subject’s relation to the symptom."),
("Reducing the patient’s symptoms quickly to secure the alliance",
"Lacanian technique does not prioritize rapid symptom relief in the preliminary phase.")],
x="Lacanian preliminary interviews (entretiens préliminaires) also serve to establish the diagnostic structure (neurosis, psychosis, perversion), because some analytic interventions can trigger psychosis, and to transform a complaint into a question addressed to the analyst (the subject supposed to know). Note that this move is contraindicated in psychosis, where questioning the subject’s position can destabilize.",
s="Lacan, The Direction of the Treatment (1958), in Écrits; Fink, A Clinical Introduction to Lacanian Psychoanalysis",
h="Rectification: “From ‘They did this to me’ to ‘What part do I play?’” Dora’s turn."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx balint flash", m="eye", l=3, hard=True,
q="Michael Balint’s work with general practitioners described the “apostolic function.” What did he mean?",
a="The doctor’s unconscious tendency to convert the patient to the doctor’s own beliefs about how to be ill and how to recover",
ae="In The Doctor, His Patient and the Illness (1957), Balint described how doctors act as if they had a mission to convert patients to their views about illness, pain and recovery. Recognizing this lets doctors meet patients where they are. He also called the doctor himself “the most frequently used drug,” whose dosage and side effects deserved study.",
w=[("The duty to teach patients about their diagnosis in plain language",
"Psychoeducation is valuable, but Balint’s apostolic function is a critique of unconscious missionary zeal."),
("The doctor’s role in religious or spiritual care",
"The term is metaphorical, not literally religious."),
("A technique of brief interpretation during short consultations",
"Balint and colleagues described a brief technique, the flash, a moment of mutual understanding in a short consultation, but that is a different concept.")],
x="Balint groups, in which clinicians discuss cases with a focus on the doctor-patient relationship, remain widespread. The flash technique, from Six Minutes for the Patient (1973), describes a sudden moment of intense understanding between doctor and patient in a brief consultation, which can be therapeutic in itself.",
s="Balint, The Doctor, His Patient and the Illness (1957); Balint & Norell, Six Minutes for the Patient (1973)",
h="Apostolic function: “the doctor as missionary.” And “the doctor is the drug”: watch the dose."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx jung word association complex indicators", m="ask", l=3, hard=True,
q="In Jung’s word association experiment, the examiner reads stimulus words and records responses. Which observation is a classic complex indicator?",
a="A markedly prolonged reaction time, often with failure to reproduce the response on retest",
ae="Jung and Riklin identified complex indicators: prolonged reaction time, no response, repetition of the stimulus word, misunderstanding, perseveration into the next response, unusual or personal responses, physical signs (laughing, blushing), and failure to reproduce the original response on the second run. Clusters of indicators point to an emotionally charged complex.",
w=[("A very fast, common response such as “table → chair”",
"Fast, common associations are the norm and indicate no complex."),
("A response that rhymes with the stimulus word",
"Clang responses can appear under emotional disturbance but are not the core indicator Jung emphasized."),
("Responding in a different language from the stimulus",
"This can happen in bilingual subjects without indicating a complex.")],
x="Jung’s association studies (1904 onward at the Burghölzli) were among the first experimental demonstrations of unconscious emotional processes and helped bring him to Freud’s attention. Combined with psychogalvanic measurements, they foreshadowed lie-detection and later implicit association research. For interviewers, the lesson is that hesitations and odd responses around particular words mark emotionally loaded territory.",
s="Jung, Studies in Word Association (1904–1910), Collected Works vol. 2",
h="Complex indicators: “the pause that betrays.” Slow, stuck, strange, or forgotten = complex nearby."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx triangle of person tcp link", m="say", l=3, hard=True,
q="A patient says you seem bored with her, just as her husband is, and just as her father always seemed when she spoke at dinner. Which intervention is a T-C-P (or T-O-P) link in Malan’s sense?",
a="“It seems the same fear lives in three places: with me here, with your husband now, and with your father long ago — the fear that your voice bores the people who matter.”",
ae="A T-C-P link connects the transference (T), current others (C or O) and past figures (P), showing the same pattern in all three. Malan found this type of interpretation strongly associated with good outcome in brief dynamic therapy.",
w=[("“I’m not bored; I’m very interested in what you have to say.”",
"Reassurance may be true, but it closes the opening rather than exploring the pattern."),
("“Your husband sounds very dismissive; have you considered couples therapy?”",
"This addresses the current relationship but drops the transference and the past."),
("“Tell me more about your father at dinnertime.”",
"Exploring the past is useful, but on its own it is not a link across the triangle.")],
x="Malan warned against premature links. They work when the patient is already feeling the pattern in the session, as here. Delivered too early, they sound like a formula and are intellectualized.",
s="Malan, Individual Psychotherapy and the Science of Psychodynamics (1979)",
h="T-C-P link: “Here, now, then” — three windows, same view."),
]
